vm=['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.html'];fa=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/posix_fadvise.2.html'];op=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/open.2.html'];mm=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/mmap.2.html'];sf=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/sendfile.2.html'];tl=['https://docs.kernel.org/networking/tls.html'];my=['https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-parameters.html#sysvar_innodb_flush_method'];pg=['https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html'];ra=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/mm/readahead.c'];pap=['https://db.cs.cmu.edu/mmap-cidr2022/']
def e(a,c,r,s=vm):edit(m,a,c,r,s)
def f(n,k,c,r,s=vm,o=None):qe(q,'fs-page-cache-q'+str(n),k,c,r,s,o)
e('Memory is roughly a thousand times faster than an SSD and a hundred thousand times faster than a disk seek.','A resident-memory access can avoid storage latency; the ratio depends on access pattern, hardware and queueing.','Unsupportedexactratios.')
e('Almost every file `read` and `write` on Linux goes through it.','Ordinary buffered regular-file I/O normally uses the page cache; direct I/O, DAX and special files have different paths.','Scopebufferedregular.',op)
e('The page cache is a set of 4 KiB memory pages, each holding one page-sized chunk of a file, indexed by (inode, offset). Since Linux 2.4 it is unified: the same pages serve `read`, `write` and `mmap`, so all three see one consistent copy.','The page cache associates file offsets with cached memory, using an inode\'s mapping and page indices. Page size is architecture dependent; modern kernels can manage multiple pages together as folios. Ordinary buffered I/O and shared file mappings use the same backing cache. Private mappings can diverge on COW writes, and concurrency still needs synchronization.','4K/alwaysconsistent/privateCOW.',mm)
e('No I/O.','No payload read from storage is needed for an up-to-date hit; metadata updates, faults in the destination buffer or other work can still occur.','HitnotnoIOofanykind.',op)
e('Clean cached pages are discarded instantly when a program needs memory.','Reclaimable clean pages can be discarded without writing their file payload back, but locking, references, scanning and other constraints mean reclamation is not instantaneous or guaranteed for every page.','Instant reclaimfalse.')
e('The first `grep -r` over a source tree reads from disk; the second hits the page cache and can be many times faster.','A run with cold file pages may need storage reads; a subsequent run can benefit from cached data and metadata if they remain resident.','No guaranteedfirstcold/secondallhit.')
e('It starts with a small window and **doubles** it while the pattern continues, up to a maximum (`read_ahead_kb`, commonly **128 KiB** by default per device).','Read-ahead grows according to kernel/filesystem heuristics and configured limits; it is not always simple doubling. Inspect the actual read_ahead_kb value instead of assuming a universal 128 KiB default.','Algorithm/versionnotfixed.',ra)
e('| `WILLNEED` | Start reading now |','| `WILLNEED` | Hint to begin bringing a range into cache |','Advisory.',fa)
e('| `DONTNEED` | Drop these pages |','| `DONTNEED` | Advise releasing unneeded cached pages; not guaranteed eviction |','Dirtypartialpagesignored.',fa)
e('without it, they push the useful hot data out of the cache.','it can reduce pollution from one-pass scans, but eviction policy and whether others need those same pages matter. Dirty or partial-page ranges may not be released.','Notguaranteefix.',fa)
e('A buffered `write` does not touch the disk:','A typical buffered write initially updates cached data, but the call can also trigger allocation, read-modify-write work, writeback or throttling. It does not promise durability on ordinary completion:','CanissueIOduringwrite.')
e('3. Return. Your data now exists only in RAM.','3. Return after copying the reported byte count. Some data may already have reached storage, but persistence is not guaranteed.','Not always onlyRAM.')
e('The two ratios are percentages of *available* memory, not total RAM.','The ratios apply to the kernel\'s dirtyable-memory accounting (free/reclaimable memory with exclusions), not simply installed RAM or the displayed MemAvailable value. Per-device and cgroup writeback also affect behavior.','Dirtyable !=MemAvailable.')
e('Normally, data reaches disk within roughly 30–35 seconds of being written.','Expiry controls eligibility for periodic writeback, not a minimum delay or durability deadline; other triggers can write earlier and congestion can delay completion.','Criticalfalse30s alwaysminimum.')
e('Misaligned I/O fails with `EINVAL`.','Requirements vary by filesystem/kernel/file and can be queried via STATX_DIOALIGN where supported. Misalignment can fail with EINVAL or fall back to buffered I/O.','Alignment/fallback.',op)
e('Buffer address, file offset and length must usually be aligned to the device\'s logical block size (often 512 B or 4 KiB).','Buffer address, file offset and length can have alignment requirements that are not universally identical to the device\'s logical block size.','Filesystemalignment.',op)
e('No read-ahead and no write-back merging, so small direct I/Os are *slower* than buffered ones unless you batch them yourself.','Direct I/O avoids ordinary page-cache hits and read-ahead. Application caching/batching, block-layer merging, device behavior and workload determine performance; it is not universally slower.','No merge/speeduniversal.',op)
e('your virtual pages **point at the page cache pages themselves**.','ordinary file-backed mappings can initially reference the file\'s cached pages; private writes can create COW copies. Language-level operations such as Python slicing may still copy data.','Pythonslice notzerocopy.',mm)
e('header = m[:16]   # page fault','header = m[:16]   # copies 16 bytes','Sliceexplicit.',mm)
e('The first touch of each page causes a **page fault**.','An access without a present usable translation causes a **page fault**. Prefaulting, fault-around and population options mean not every first page touch necessarily faults separately.','Faultaround.',mm)
e('| Cost per access | syscall | none once mapped |','| Access path | syscall per read call, then loads from buffer | ordinary loads once mapped |','Notreadsyscallperbyte.',mm)
e('| Control of eviction | n/a | kernel decides |','| Eviction | kernel policy, advisory hints | kernel policy, advisory hints |','Readcachekerneltoo.',mm)
e('forces expensive cross-CPU interrupts.','may require translation invalidation and inter-CPU coordination; exact mechanisms and costs vary.','NoteverycoreIPI.',pap)
e('which is fatal for a database that must write its log first.','so a database must enforce WAL ordering before dirtying shared mapped data or use a design that preserves ordering through another mechanism. This is a design constraint, not proof that every mmap database is incorrect.','CanfsyncWALbeforemodify.',pap)
e('That\'s four copies, two of them by the CPU, plus two system calls per chunk.','In this simplified uncached conventional path there are two CPU copies plus device transfers, with read/send calls per chunk. Actual copy and syscall counts depend on cache state, short transfers, offloads and implementation.','Countsmodel.',sf)
e('No CPU copy is left, which is why it\'s called **zero-copy**. Without scatter-gather there is one CPU copy, into the socket buffer, which is still half the CPU copying.','This can eliminate payload copies through user space and, with a suitable implementation, CPU payload copies on the transmit path. The exact copying depends on offloads, transforms and fallback paths; zero-copy is a path capability, not a universal count guarantee.','NICscattergatheralone insufficient.',sf)
e('**Kafka** serves consumers with `sendfile` (Java\'s `FileChannel.transferTo`), which is a big part of why brokers can saturate network links with modest CPU.','**Kafka** can use file-transfer paths for suitable plaintext log delivery; encryption, conversion and configuration can select other paths.','Removeunmeasuredsaturationclaim.',sf)
e('Buffered I/O hides latency for *reads that hit* and *all writes*. A read that misses still blocks the thread.','Buffered hits can avoid payload-storage latency. Writes can block on throttling, allocation or I/O, and synchronous read misses can wait for data.','Allwritesnonblockingfalse.')
e('- Buffered writes only dirty pages in RAM; flusher threads write them back after about 30 s or under memory pressure, and writers are throttled at `dirty_ratio`.','- Buffered writes initially update cached pages but can block or trigger I/O; expiry is writeback eligibility, not a durability deadline. Dirty thresholds influence throttling.','Summaryno30s guarantee.')
e('- `mmap` avoids a copy but brings page faults, `SIGBUS` on errors and no control over write-back.','- mmap can avoid read-buffer copies but needs fault/error handling and a design that accounts for shared-page writeback.','Noabsolutecontrol.',mm)
e('## What the page cache is','> [!note] Performance evidence gap\n> No benchmark record accompanies the latency ratios or workload speed claims in this lesson. Fixed ratios and guaranteed timing outcomes are omitted; numeric scenarios below are illustrative models.\n\n## What the page cache is','Visiblegapmeasurement.')
f(1,'text','The host reports about 24 GiB available without swapping; low free alone does not establish host memory exhaustion','Hostfine notallconstraints.',o=1)
f(1,'explanation','MemAvailable estimates free and reclaimable memory. It is useful evidence, but cgroup limits, fragmentation and workload pressure can still constrain allocations.','Availabilitynotguarantee.',o=1)
f(1,'explanation','Low completely unused memory is not sufficient evidence of exhaustion. Reclaimable cache can be reused, though not necessarily instantly.','Instantfalse.',o=0)
f(1,'explanation','Clean reclaimable disk-backed cache does not need swapping. Anonymous memory and tmpfs/shmem can use swap; it is not limited to heap and stack.','Tmpfsswap.',o=3)
f(1,'workedExample','This illustrative summary reports substantial MemAvailable, which estimates capacity usable without swapping. Free/used/cache column definitions vary with procps versions; used is not simply live application memory, and cache is not all instantly reclaimable. Correlate the alert with PSI, cgroup limits, swap and reclaim statistics. No exact microsecond reclamation time or diagnosis of the alert\'s implementation follows from this display.','Metrics/diagnosis fixed.')
f(2,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[1]['prompt'].replace('What most likely happened to the line?','What can be concluded about the line\'s durability?'),'No unsupportedprobability.')
f(2,'text','Its survival is not guaranteed: it may have persisted through another writeback trigger or may be lost','Not mostlikelylost30s.',o=3)
f(2,'explanation','Correct. Dirty expiry does not prevent earlier writeback and ordinary write completion is not a persistence guarantee.','Eligibilitynotminimum.',o=3)
f(2,'explanation','Periodic wakeup and expiry are only part of writeback policy. Neither establishes that this write is durable after five seconds.','Not onlyoldpages.',o=1)
f(2,'explanation','Neither an exact four-byte prefix nor all-or-nothing survival follows from this program. Page-sized cache accounting does not guarantee atomic power-fail writes.','Tornwritepossible.',o=2)
f(2,'workedExample','The literal order 42 followed by newline is **9 bytes**. A successful full buffered write updates cached file data; writeback may happen before five seconds through several paths, or may not complete. dirty_expire_centisecs marks eligibility for periodic flushing, not a mandatory wait. Check the byte count, then successful fsync (and directory sync when needed) before acknowledging durability under the storage contract.','30swaitfalse.')
f(3,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[2]['prompt']+' In this hypothetical measurement, tracing confirms the first run reads file payload from storage and the second serves it from resident page cache.','Timingalone notprove.')
f(3,'explanation','Device caching can affect timing, but the supplied trace specifically identifies host page-cache hits. No universal SSD-cache capacity or speedup threshold is implied.','Falseuniversalhardwarecap.',o=1)
f(3,'explanation','The trace identifies kernel page-cache residency, not a CPU-cache-resident entire source tree. CPU-cache capacities depend on the actual processor.','No tensMBallCPU.',o=3)
f(4,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[3]['prompt'].replace('available memory','dirtyable memory in a simplified threshold model')+' Assume a 12.8 GiB backlog, no new writes, and constant end-to-end drain throughput.','Thresholdnotexactdisplay/changingwork.')
f(4,'explanation','This uses all 64 GiB rather than the stipulated 12.8 GiB backlog. Real dirty limits and throttling are dynamic, not a strict statement that exactly 20% can ever be dirty.','Not hardcap.',o=2)
f(5,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[4]['prompt'].replace('What is the most likely cause and fix?','If tracing confirms application refaults caused by cache pressure, which mitigation should be tested?'),'Cannot inferfromidleCPU.')
f(5,'explanation','A larger readahead window can increase cache pressure, but effects depend on access and reclamation; it is not guaranteed to worsen every workload.','Notguaranteedspeedimpact.',fa,o=0)
f(5,'explanation','Ordinary reads do not automatically take application advisory locks, but internal locking and filesystem-specific waits still exist. The supplied evidence identifies cache pressure.','Not no locking ever.',fa,o=1)
f(5,'explanation','Correct as a mitigation to test. DONTNEED is advisory, can affect other users of shared cached data and may not evict dirty or partial pages. Direct I/O is another option with compatibility/alignment tradeoffs.','Notreliableguaranteedfix.',fa,o=2)
f(5,'explanation','Reading file payload does not normally dirty that payload, though atime/metadata or other output can become dirty. The stated evidence is cache pressure.','Reads can dirtymetadata.',fa,o=3)
f(5,'workedExample',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[4]['workedExample'].replace('Those 2 TB will never be read again, so caching them is pure waste.','The backup may not reuse these pages, though other applications could.').replace('explicit hints are the reliable fix.','hints are advisory; validate their effect and avoid discarding shared hot ranges.'),'Neverreadagain/guarantee.',fa)
f(6,'text','Direct I/O can impose file/system-specific buffer, offset and length alignment requirements','Notdeviceonly.',op,o=0)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. STATX_DIOALIGN can report requirements where supported; misaligned requests may fail or fall back depending on the filesystem.','Fallback.',op,o=0)
f(6,'text','It can avoid the ordinary page-cache read-ahead and repeated cache-hit benefit, so application caching/batching becomes important','Unprovenusuallyslower replacedmechanism.',op,o=3)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. Performance depends on request sizes, queue depth and workload. The block layer/device can still merge or optimize requests.','Nomergingfalse.',op,o=3)
f(6,'workedExample','Direct I/O changes caching behavior, with filesystem-specific support and alignment constraints. It can reduce duplicate buffering for an application cache, but does not ensure metadata/device-cache durability. It bypasses ordinary page-cache hit/readahead behavior; application batching and device-level optimizations still matter. It is not itself a compression request.','Fixallalignment/nomerge.',op)
f(7,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[6]['prompt']+' Also assume each read returns a full 64 KiB chunk until EOF and the conventional two-CPU-copy socket path.','Shortreads affectsyscallcount.',sf)
f(7,'workedExample',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[6]['workedExample'].replace('and with scatter-gather NICs no CPU copy at all.','and suitable kernel/NIC paths can avoid payload CPU copies; short returns and offloads determine actual counts.'),'Not NICalone guarantees.',sf)
f(9,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[8]['prompt'].replace('What is the best explanation and fix?','Which cache-related explanation and configuration experiment are relevant, subject to measuring other memory consumers?'),'Notautomaticallydiagnosis.',my)
f(9,'text','Buffered I/O can duplicate data between InnoDB and page cache; evaluate supported O_DIRECT configuration and total memory budgeting','Not everypagealwaysduplicate/onlycache.',my,o=0)
f(9,'explanation','Correct. Direct data-file I/O can reduce duplicate buffering, but does not eliminate every cache, non-pool allocation or cause of swapping. Measure pressure and workload performance.','Not cureallswap.',my,o=0)
f(9,'workedExample','In the default16KiB InnoDB page-size configuration, buffered data-file reads can leave copies in both the buffer pool and page cache. Reclaim decisions can swap anonymous pool memory under pressure. Supported direct-I/O settings can reduce that duplication, but the process still has other allocations and the kernel retains other caches. Budget all consumers and validate performance. PostgreSQL documentation presents25% shared_buffers as a starting point for a dedicated server, not a universal optimum.','Noalwaysonlycache/defaultpage scope.',my+pg)
f(10,'explanation','A design that dirties shared mapped pages before making the matching WAL durable risks violating WAL ordering. Persisting WAL before modifying shared data or using separate buffers is an extra protocol that can address it.','Not mmapcannotenforce.',pap,o=0)
f(10,'workedExample',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[9]['workedExample'].replace('every CPU that might have the mapping cached must flush it, via inter-processor interrupts.','relevant stale translations must be invalidated; the kernel can use different targeting/batching mechanisms, not necessarily an IPI to every core.'),'IPInotalwaysall.',pap)
f(11,'explanation','With compatible versions, TLS ciphers and configuration, kTLS can move record processing into the kernel/offload path and allow sendfile integration. It does not promise zero CPU work or universally zero copies.','ktlsrequirements.',tl,o=2)
f(11,'workedExample',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[10]['workedExample'].replace('Plain-HTTP static serving gets zero-copy for free; HTTPS needs kTLS to keep it.','Plaintext serving can use an optimized sendfile path; HTTPS needs compatible TLS integration such as kTLS to avoid the usual user-space plaintext read path. Exact copy counts and offload benefits depend on configuration.'),'Notfree/noCPUguarantee.',tl)
f(12,'explanation','Correct as a tuning direction to test. Size dirty budgets from measured throughput and latency targets, with bytes settings overriding their ratio counterparts. This limits accumulation but does not guarantee short stalls during slowdowns or continued overload.','Not fixedthresholdguarantees.',o=3)
f(12,'workedExample','Installed256GiB is not necessarily the dirtyable-memory denominator. In a simplified256GiB dirtyable model,10% and20% give25.6 and51.2GiB. Background writeback and throttling also depend on per-device/cgroup state. A2GiB backlog at a constant1GB/s takes about2.15s to drain with no new writes; that is an arithmetic scenario, not a hard stall bound. Test lower byte-based budgets while measuring throughput/latency; setting a bytes knob disables its corresponding ratio.','Dirtyabledenom/stallboundfalse.')
